
Taking a yearly break after university has become a preference for some students. This essay will examine the benefits and downsides of this pathway, and argue that while there are some inevitable setbacks, the advantages of this approach still outweigh the disadvantages to certain extent.
On the first hand, an additional year before university provides young people with sufficient time to re-discover themselves and the world. After a long period of time finishing the coursework, catching up with their peers or cramming for exams, a gap year is far more necessary and valuable for students to be reflective on their past achievements and future directions. On the other hand, this additional time can also be used for rest and relaxation. Nowadays, hustle culture is a pivotal issue that can easily cause people to burn out, suffer with anxiety disorder, or other health-related issues due to a busy lifestyle. By finally having time for themselves - traveling or learning new things - students can develop a well-regulated nervous system that is less prone to these mentioned issues.
This approach, however, is not without drawbacks. An inevitable consequence of this pathway is that it creates a certain financial burden to the students' families and society. During this time, these young people don't have explicit productive values, because their skills and knowledge acquired from university can not be put to use. This isn't even a choice for low-income family, where all the savings have been invested into their children's education. Furthermore, on a practical aspect, if young individuals just blindly follow this pathway without careful considerations on their goals, or don't have a strong and determined mindset, they can easily feel left behind by their peers, lost their momentum, and become hesitate to enter the workforce in the future.
In conclusion, although the decision to take a gap year after university has certain merits, it also comes with tremendous drawbacks if not being taken seriously. From my perspective, I firmly believe that the choice should be made carefully and thoughfully, considering one's background, purpose and mindset.

- be reflective on their past achievements and future directions, discover themselves and the world
- rest and relaxation -> hustle culture can make young people easily prone to burn out, anxiety disorder, or other health-related issues due to a busy lifestyle
- financial burden to their family and society because they don't have productive values, especially in low-income family
- don't have a strong, determined mindset -> experience peer pressure
